00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:19.400
it, and then you having to go to your production
server, and make those same changes manually,

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:23.650
you're going to write these migration files
that are going to describe your database.

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:29.610
Now, this is a little bit of a higher concept.
But just think of a migration as a file that

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:34.760
holds all the instructions that you need to
tell your database to create itself. And so

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:40.680
throughout this project, we're going to modify
the database, but through the migration, not

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:45.630
directly into the database. Now, why would
you do it this way? Well, the answer is fairly

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:52.510
simple. It describes each change in a very
systematic way, you know, if you were to just

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:57.490
go into your database and make some changes,
unless you document those very, very well,

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:01.830
more than likely, you're gonna forget to run
a step or two in your production, and then

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:06.110
everything blows up. Furthermore, any other
developers working with, you are not going

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:11.480
to know how to recreate that change. And so
that is not great for teams. So things like

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:16.410
migrations are one of those integral parts
of working in a team, because it allows everybody

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:22.400
